s="\"A Matroid Generalization of a Result on Row-Latin Rectangles (https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9807036)\\nciteturn0academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 28.4 years ago; Title: A Matroid Generalization of a Result on Row-Latin Rectangles ... Chappell ... Drisko showed that, if m \\\\ge 2n-1, then A has a transversal: a set of n distinct entries with no two in the same row or column. ... Lastly, we observe that m \\\\ge 2n-1 appears to force the existence of not merely one but many transversals.\\nTitle: A Matroid Generalization of a Result on Row-Latin Rectangles\\nAuthors: Glenn G. Chappell\\nDate: Wed Jul  8 17:12:50 1998\\n\\nLet A be an m \\\\times n matrix in which the entries of each row are all distinct. Drisko showed that, if m \\\\ge 2n-1, then A has a transversal: a set of n distinct entries with no two in the same row or column. We generalize this to matrices with entries in a matroid. For such a matrix A, we show that if each row of A forms an independent set, then we can require the transversal to be independent as well. We determine the complexity of an algorithm based on the proof of this result. Lastly, we observe that m \\\\ge 2n-1 appears to force the existence of not merely one but many transversals. We discuss a number of conjectures related to this observation (some of which involve matroids and some of which do not).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nA MATROIDAL GENERALIZATION OF RESULTS OF DRISKO (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=c06fae09ce96e7cc9de60770c0ac3cab124cb436&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\\nciteturn0search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; set of a matroid M, and whose rows are independent sets in M, has a partial\\ntransversal of size n which is an independent set of M. ... N to be partition matroids, and Theorem 1.2 follows in the case that one of the\\n2\\nWe provide some deﬁnitions and notation concerning matroids. For a set A and\\nan element x we use the notation A + x for A ∪{x} and A −x for A \\\\ {x}. A\\ncollection M of subsets of a ground set S is a matroid if it is hereditary and it\\nsatisﬁes an augmentation property: If A, B ∈M and |B| > |A|, then there exists\\nx ∈B \\\\ A such that A + x ∈M. Sets in M are called independent and subsets of\\nS not belonging to M are called dependent. An element x ∈S is spanned by A if\\neither x ∈A or I+x ̸∈M for some independent set I ⊆A. The set of elements that\\nare spanned in M by A is denoted by spM(A). A circuit is a minimal dependent\\nset. For more background on matroid theory the reader is referred to in the books\\nof Oxley [4] and Welsh [5].\\nChappell [2] proved the following generalization of Drisko’s theorem:\\nTheorem 1.2. Any (2n −1) × n array whose entries are taken from the ground\\nset of a matroid M, and whose rows are independent sets in M, has a partial\\ntransversal of size n which is an independent set of M.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nThe cones: \\\\(\\\\mathcal{C}^{n+1}_{\\\\mathcal{E}}\\\\) is the smallest closed convex cone containing the flag vectors of all Eulerian posets. (https://bayer.ku.edu/Eulerian/cone.pdf)\\nciteturn0search14 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 months ago; \\\\(\\\\mathcal{C}^{n+1}_{\\\\mathcal{D}}\\\\) is the smallest closed convex cone containing the flag vectors of the horizontal doubles of all half-Eulerian posets. ... Let \\\\(\\\\mathcal{V}\\\\) be a set of vectors contained in the cone of half-Eulerian flag \\\\(f\\\\)-vectors, and let \\\\(\\\\mathrm{cone}(\\\\mathcal{V})\\\\) be the smallest convex cone containing \\\\(\\\\mathcal{V}\\\\).\\nThe cones: \\\\(\\\\mathcal{C}^{n+1}_{\\\\mathcal{E}}\\\\) is the smallest closed convex cone containing the flag vectors of all Eulerian posets.\\n\\n\\\\(\\\\mathcal{C}^{n+1}_{\\\\mathcal{D}}\\\\) is the smallest closed convex cone containing the flag vectors of the horizontal doubles of all half-Eulerian posets. (See Section 2 of our paper.)\\n\\n## 2 From PORTA output to Eulerian cone\\n\\nLet \\\\(\\\\mathcal{V}\\\\) be a set of vectors contained in the cone of half-Eulerian flag \\\\(f\\\\)-vectors, and let \\\\(\\\\mathrm{cone}(\\\\mathcal{V})\\\\) be the smallest convex cone containing \\\\(\\\\mathcal{V}\\\\). Consider the affine transformation \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\) that multiplies each \\\\(f_S\\\\)-entry of a vector by \\\\(2^{|S|}\\\\), and divides the coefficient of \\\\(f_S\\\\) in an inequality by \\\\(2^{|S|}\\\\). Let \\\\(\\\\mathcal{K}^{n+1}\\\\) be the image of \\\\(\\\\mathrm{cone}(\\\\mathcal{V})\\\\) under \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\). Then \\\\(\\\\mathcal{K}^{n+1}\\\\) is contained in the closed cone \\\\(\\\\mathcal{C}^{n+1}_{\\\\mathcal{D}}\\\\) of flag \\\\(f\\\\)-vectors of horizontal doubles of half-Eulerian posets, which is contained in the Eulerian cone \\\\(\\\\mathcal{C}^{n+1}_{\\\\mathcal{E}}\\\\).\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nFlag Vectors of Eulerian Partially Ordered Sets (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0195669800904141)\\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; The closed cone of flag vectors of Eulerian partially ordered sets is studied. ... Certain limit posets of Billera and Hetyei are half-Eulerian; they give rise to extreme rays of the cone for Eulerian posets.\\n\\n## European Journal of Combinatorics\\n\\nVolume 22, Issue 1, January 2001, Pages 5-26\\n\\n# \\n\\nRegular Article\\n\\nFlag Vectors of Eulerian Partially Ordered Sets\\n\\nhttps://doi.org/10.1006/eujc.2000.0414Get rights and content\\n\\nUnder an Elsevier user license\\n\\nOpen archive\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nThe closed cone of flag vectors of Eulerian partially ordered sets is studied. A new family of linear inequalities valid for Eulerian flag vectors is given. Half-Eulerian posets are defined. Certain limit posets of Billera and Hetyei are half-Eulerian; they give rise to extreme rays of the cone for Eulerian posets. Other extreme posets are formed from consideration of the cd -index. The cone of Eulerian flag vectors is completely determined up through rank seven.\\n\\n\\nOn leave from the Alfréd Rényi Institute of Mathematics, Hungarian Academy of Sciences. Current address: Department of Mathematics, University of North Carolina at Charlotte, Charlotte, NC 28223-9998.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nGlenn G Chappell - Research Profile (https://independent.academia.edu/GlennChappell3)\\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; A Matroid Generalization of a Result on Row-Latin Rectangles ... Drisko [4] showed that, if m ≥ 2n -1, then A has a transversal : a set of n distinct entries with no two in the same row or column. ... Lastly, we observe that m ≥ 2n -1 appears to force the existence of not merely one but many transversals. ... Chappell, Randall Dougherty, Roger B.\\nformat_quoteEvery bipartite planar graph is (a:b)-choosable if and only if ab ≥ 3, improving previous results.format_quote\\n\\ndownloadDownload free PDF\\n\\nA Matroid Generalization of a Result on Row-Latin Rectangles\\n\\narXiv (Cornell University), Jul 8, 1998\\n\\nLet A be an m × n matrix in which the entries of each row are all distinct. Drisko [4] showed tha... more Let A be an m × n matrix in which the entries of each row are all distinct. Drisko [4] showed that, if m ≥ 2n -1, then A has a transversal : a set of n distinct entries with no two in the same row or column. We generalize this to matrices with entries in a matroid. For such a matrix A, we show that if each row of A forms an independent set, then we can require the transversal to be independent as well. We determine the complexity of an algorithm based on the proof of this result. Lastly, we observe that m ≥ 2n -1 appears to force the existence of not merely one but many transversals. We discuss a number of conjectures related to this observation (some of which involve matroids and some of which do not).\\n\\nProblems and Solutions\\n\\nThe American Mathematical Monthly, 2000\\n\\nwith the collaboration of Paul T. Bateman, Mario Benedicty, Paul Bracken, Duane M. Broline, Ezra ... more with the collaboration of Paul T. Bateman, Mario Benedicty, Paul Bracken, Duane M. Broline, Ezra A. Brown, RichardT. Bumby, Glenn G. Chappell, Randall Dougherty, Roger B. Eggleton, Kevin Ford, Ira M. Gessel, Bart Goddard, Jerrold R. Griggs, Douglas A. Hensley, John R. Isbell, Kiran S. Kedlaya, Murray S. Klamkin, Fred Kochman, Frederick W. Luttmann, Vania Mascioni, Frank B. Miles, Richard Pfiefer, Cecil C. Rousseau, Leonard Smiley, John Henry Steelman, Kenneth Stolarsky, Richard Stong, Charles Vanden Eynden, and William E. Watkins.\\n\\nColoring with no 2-colored 's\\n\\nColoring with no 2-Colored P\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nMatroid Theory (https://www.math.ens.psl.eu/~benoist/refs/Oxley.pdf)\\nciteturn0search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.5 years ago; **Conjecture 15.9.1**  *Let* \\\\(B_1, B_2, \\\\ldots, B_n\\\\) *be disjoint bases of a rank-\\\\(n\\\\) matroid* \\\\(M\\\\). ... For example, Geelen and Humphries (2006) proved the conjecture when \\\\(M\\\\) is a paving matroid; for an odd prime \\\\(p\\\\), Drisko (1997) and Glynn (2010) proved the conjecture for \\\\(n\\\\) in \\\\(\\\\{p+1, p-1\\\\}\\\\) when \\\\(M\\\\) is \\\\(\\\\mathbb{F}\\\\)-representable and the characteristic of \\\\(\\\\mathbb{F}\\\\) is zero, or is not in some finite set of primes, or is \\\\(p\\\\) when \\\\(n = p-1\\\\); Aharoni and Berger (2006) proved that there is a partition of \\\\(B_1 \\\\cup B_2 \\\\cup \\\\cdots \\\\cup B_n\\\\) into at most \\\\(2n\\\\) sets each of which is a partial transversal of \\\\((B_1, B_2, \\\\ldots, B_n)\\\\) that is independent in \\\\(M\\\\); and Geelen and Webb (2007) proved two other partial results including the existence of at least \\\\(\\\\lfloor \\\\sqrt{n} \\\\rfloor\\\\) disjoint transversals of \\\\((B_1, B_2, \\\\ldots, B_n)\\\\) that are bases of \\\\(M\\\\).\\n604  *Unsolved problems*\\n\\n## 15.9  A miscellany\\n\\nWe conclude this chapter with a collection of problems that do not seem to belong in any of the earlier sections. We begin with yet another conjecture of Rota (in Huang and Rota 1994), which is known as Rota’s Basis Conjecture.\\n\\n**Conjecture 15.9.1**  *Let* \\\\(B_1, B_2, \\\\ldots, B_n\\\\) *be disjoint bases of a rank-\\\\(n\\\\) matroid* \\\\(M\\\\). *Then the family* \\\\((B_1, B_2, \\\\ldots, B_n)\\\\) *has \\\\(n\\\\) disjoint transversals each of which is a basis of* \\\\(M\\\\). □\\n\\nEquivalently, the \\\\(n^2\\\\) elements of \\\\(B_1 \\\\cup B_2 \\\\cup \\\\cdots \\\\cup B_n\\\\) can be arranged as entries of an \\\\(n \\\\times n\\\\) matrix so that the entries in row \\\\(i\\\\) are the elements of \\\\(B_i\\\\), and the entries in each column form a basis of \\\\(M\\\\). By the basis exchange axiom, the conjecture holds for \\\\(n = 2\\\\), and Chan (1995) proved it for \\\\(n = 3\\\\). Several authors have proved partial results towards the conjecture. For example, Geelen and Humphries (2006) proved the conjecture when \\\\(M\\\\) is a paving matroid; for an odd prime \\\\(p\\\\), Drisko (1997) and Glynn (2010) proved the conjecture for \\\\(n\\\\) in \\\\(\\\\{p+1, p-1\\\\}\\\\) when \\\\(M\\\\) is \\\\(\\\\mathbb{F}\\\\)-representable and the characteristic of \\\\(\\\\mathbb{F}\\\\) is zero, or is not in some finite set of primes, or is \\\\(p\\\\) when \\\\(n = p-1\\\\); Aharoni and Berger (2006) proved that there is a partition of \\\\(B_1 \\\\cup B_2 \\\\cup \\\\cdots \\\\cup B_n\\\\) into at most \\\\(2n\\\\) sets each of which is a partial transversal of \\\\((B_1, B_2, \\\\ldots, B_n)\\\\) that is independent in \\\\(M\\\\); and Geelen and Webb (2007) proved two other partial results including the existence of at least \\\\(\\\\lfloor \\\\sqrt{n} \\\\rfloor\\\\) disjoint transversals of \\\\((B_1, B_2, \\\\ldots, B_n)\\\\) that are bases of \\\\(M\\\\). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nOn Rota's Basis Conjecture (https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/060666494)\\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 19.0 years ago; Crawled: 2.4 years ago; # On Rota's Basis Conjecture ... Rota conjectured that if $(B_1,\\\\ldots,B_n)$ are disjoint bases in a rank-n matroid M, then there are n disjoint transversals of $(B_1,\\\\ldots,B_n)$ that are bases of M. ... We also prove that if $(B_1,\\\\ldots,B_k)$ are disjoint bases of a rank-n matroid with $n> \\\\binom{k+1}{2}$, then there are n disjoint independent transversals of $(B_1,\\\\ldots,B_k)$.\\n\\n# On Rota's Basis Conjecture\\n\\nAuthors: Jim Geelen and Kerri WebbAuthors Info & Affiliations\\n\\nhttps://doi.org/10.1137/060666494\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nRota conjectured that if $(B_1,\\\\ldots,B_n)$ are disjoint bases in a rank-n matroid M, then there are n disjoint transversals of $(B_1,\\\\ldots,B_n)$ that are bases of M. We prove the weaker result that there are $O(\\\\sqrt n)$ disjoint transversals of $(B_1,\\\\ldots,B_n)$ that are bases. We also prove that if $(B_1,\\\\ldots,B_k)$ are disjoint bases of a rank-n matroid with $n> \\\\binom{k+1}{2}$, then there are n disjoint independent transversals of $(B_1,\\\\ldots,B_k)$.\\n\\n### MSC codes\\n\\n  1. 05B35\\n\\n### Keywords\\n\\n  1. matroids\\n  2. Rado's theorem\\n  3. Rota's basis conjecture\\n\\n## References\\n\\n1.\\n\\nR. Huang and G.-C. Rota, On the relations of various conjectures on Latin squares and straightening coefficients, Discrete Math., 128 (1994), pp. 225–236.\\n\\nCrossref\\n\\nWeb of Science\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\ndoi:10.1016/j.aam.2008.05.002 (https://www.math.uwaterloo.ca/~jfgeelen/Publications/uniform.pdf)\\nciteturn0search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 18.1 years ago; Crawled: 18.1 years ago; If Rota’s Conjecture holds, then, for any finite field F, it takes only O(1) oracle calls to certify non-representability over F. ... A matroid M is an intertwine of two matroids N_1 and N_2 if M contains an N_1- and an N_2-minor but no proper minor of M does. ... For example, much of the work in proving Seymour’s Decomposition Theorem for regular matroids [14] is in finding the 3-connected regular intertwines of M(K_{3,3}) and M(K_{3,3})*. ... If each element of λ(S_1) and λ(S_2) is in a dependent transversal and neither λ(S_1) nor λ(S_2) is isomorphic to a minor of the other, then there exist arbitrarily large spikes that are intertwines of λ(S_1) and λ(S_2).\\nJ. Geelen / Advances in Applied Mathematics 41 (2008) 628–637\\n\\nConjecture 4.4 has received very little attention, and may be easy.\\n\\nSeymour [15] proved that there is no polynomial-time algorithm for determining whether or not a matroid, given by its rank oracle, is binary. His proof used spikes; there is a unique binary spike of each rank. For each n ≥ 3, let S_n = (E, 𝔽) where E = {1, ..., n} and 𝔽 contains all odd subsets of E. Then λ(S_n), up to isomorphism, the unique binary rank-n spike. There are 2^{n−1} dependent transversals, and relaxing any one of these yields a non-binary spike. Therefore to verify that a rank-n spike is binary we need at least 2^{n−1} oracle calls. It is easy to prove similar results for all other fields.\\n\\nCertifying non-representability seems to be easier. If Rota’s Conjecture holds, then, for any finite field F, it takes only O(1) oracle calls to certify non-representability over F. Geelen, Gerards, and Whittle [5] have proved that, for any finite field F of prime order, certifying non-F-representability requires only O(n^2) rank evaluations for matroids on n elements.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nFlag vectors of Eulerian partially ordered sets (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=a9e86647fdb3c33ddae44fadca71b310881d8b1